To convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector`, you can simply copy the characters of the string into the vector. Here is an example of how you can do this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::string str = "hello";
    std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());

    // Print the elements of the vector
    for (char c : vec) {
        std::cout << c << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we create a `std::vector<char>` and initialize it using the range constructor, passing `str.begin()` and `str.end()` as arguments. This copies all the characters of the string `str` to the vector `vec`.

